  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1223 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nMonday through Friday:\n\nHigh pressure should slowly push east Monday morning, keeping\nthe morning hours mostly dry. By noon warm advection is \nexpected to surge north between the exiting high pressure \nsystem, and an occluding low pressure system over the central to\nnorthern Great Plains. A swath of rain will ride the WAA wave\ninto Wisconsin and Monday afternoon looks to feature some steady\nlight to moderate rain, with some heavier rain tied to\nembedded thunderstorms, as a few hundred J/kg of MUCAPE will \narrive with the rain. Some of the downpours with the embedded \nthunderstorms could be heavy, as PWATs of 1.7 to 1.8 inches are \nexpected to arrive Monday afternoon. It does look like earlier \nconcerns for severe weather have lessened as the warm sector of \nthe occluding low should stay to our west in Iowa while we see \nweak elevated convection through the afternoon and early \nevening.\n\nBy Monday night, the more defined warm sector from earlier in\nthe day will give way into an elongated baroclinic zone. This\nbaroclinic zone will then sink south-southeast as a cold front\nfrom early morning Tuesday to midday. Convection is expected to\nlight up along the front overnight, and track from southwest to\nnortheast along the sagging front. With the high PWATs mentioned\nbefore, these storms may cause some heavy rainfall. An aggregate\nof the CAMs, deterministic models, and GFS and ECMWF ensembles\ngenerally show 0.5 to 2 inches of accumulation on the lower and\nupper ends of the envelopes from Monday Afternoon through \nTuesday morning, but locally higher amounts may occur where \nstorms track repeatedly. Some gusty winds can't be ruled out\nwith these storms Monday night into Tuesday morning, given the \nwater load in the storms and more MUCAPE near 1000 J/kg over the\narea.\n\nLighter, showery rain may then linger through Tuesday afternoon\nbefore the front completely pushes rain south of the WI/IL\nborder and the front stalls over northern IL. We'll have a \nbreak Wednesday morning, before the stationary front wiggles\nnorth a bit, bringing some rain chances for Wednesday afternoon\ninto Thursday morning. Details get murky beyond, but it does\nappear that some shower chances may linger into Friday, with the\nNBM painting broad PoPs over Wisconsin from 35 to 55% through\nthe day.\n\nCMiller",
